// M9 correctly rounded binary64 sin, cos, and tan.
//
// Reduction. |x| <= pi/4 is used as is. Above that, x = M 2^E with M a 53-bit
// integer is reduced by Payne-Hanek: x (2/pi) = q + f with q an integer and
// |f| <= 1/2, and r = f pi/2. Only q mod 4 and f matter, so the product is
// formed with five 64-bit words of 2/pi starting at word k0 = floor((E-2)/64):
// every earlier word contributes a multiple of 4. The 384-bit product is
// exact; omitting the words after the fifth leaves it below the true value by
// under 2^53 2^(E - 64 k0 - 320) <= 2^-202 absolute.
//
// tools/m9/generate-trig-constants.py establishes, by a continued-fraction
// search over every exponent, that x (2/pi) is never within 2^-61.5 of an
// integer for binary64 x >= pi/4 (the closest is 6381956970095103 2^797), so
// |f| > 2^-61.5 and the omitted words cost under 2^-140.5 relative. Keeping
// 128 bits of f, the 128-bit pi/2, and the product f pi/2 add 2^-127 each, so
// r has relative error below 2^-125.4.
//
// Evaluation, at |r| <= pi/4, z = r^2 <= 0.617:
//
//     sin r = r * sum_{k=0..15} (-1)^k z^k / (2k+1)!,  tail < 2^-133.9 relative,
//     cos r =     sum_{k=0..16} (-1)^k z^k / (2k)!,    tail < 2^-139.1 relative.
//
// A running error bound over the Horner steps at z = (pi/4)^2, each truncating
// by at most 2^-127 relative, gives under 2^-125.7 for the sine sum and
// 2^-125.1 for the cosine. A relative error e in r moves sin r by at most e
// relative and cos r by at most r tan(r) e <= 0.79 e. So sin and cos are
// within 2^-124.3 relative, and tan = sin / cos (or -cos / sin), with the
// division's 2^-124.6, within 2^-122.8, against the 2^-116 certification
// margin.
//
// Closed forms: for nonzero |x| < 2^-27, sin(x) = x - x^3/6 and
// tan(x) = x + x^3/3 lie within x^2/3 < 2^-54 relative of x, strictly toward
// and away from zero respectively, and cos(x) = 1 - x^2/2 lies strictly
// between 1 - 2^-55 and 1; the same holds for cos r on a cosine branch
// after reduction, which the hardest arguments need. sin, cos, and tan of a nonzero algebraic number
// are transcendental (Lindemann-Weierstrass), so no other result is on the
// binary64 grid or a midpoint.

// Generated by tools/m9/generate-trig-constants.py. Do not edit by hand.
// 2/pi to 1280 bits, cross-checked against mpmath; word k holds bits
// 64k+1 .. 64k+64 after the binary point, truncated.
constant ulong SOFT_TRIG_TWO_OVER_PI[20] = {
    0xa2f9836e4e441529ul, 0xfc2757d1f534ddc0ul,
    0xdb6295993c439041ul, 0xfe5163abdebbc561ul,
    0xb7246e3a424dd2e0ul, 0x06492eea09d1921cul,
    0xfe1deb1cb129a73eul, 0xe88235f52ebb4484ul,
    0xe99c7026b45f7e41ul, 0x3991d639835339f4ul,
    0x9c845f8bbdf9283bul, 0x1ff897ffde05980ful,
    0xef2f118b5a0a6d1ful, 0x6d367ecf27cb09b7ul,
    0x4f463f669e5fea2dul, 0x7527bac7ebe5f17bul,
    0x3d0739f78a5292eaul, 0x6bfb5fb11f8d5d08ul,
    0x56033046fc7b6babul, 0xf0cfbc209af4361dul,
};

